.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
data/
*.egg-info/
.env
_app.out.log
_app.err.log
_serve.out.log
_serve.err.log
*.log

# One-off probes, backups and stray shell-redirect artifacts, all of which live
# at the repo root. Scoped to the root with a leading slash so real files in
# subdirectories still get tracked.
/_*

# ...but not the test harnesses, which the rule above swallowed whole when it
# was added. They are the verification suite - budget 48 checks, phone 41,
# desktop client 30, the registry's fail-closed store guard - not scratch work.
# Leaving them untracked meant the one thing that proves a change is safe lived
# only on whichever machine happened to run it last.
!/_*_test.py
!/_*_test.mjs
!/_route_e2e.py

# Nor the negative controls, which are the tests ON the tests: they mutate a source
# file, require the suite to catch it, and restore. Deliberately NOT named *_test.py,
# because a runner that globs the suite must not pick them up - each one runs the
# whole suite several times over. They belong in the repo for the same reason the
# suite does: "the test passed" means nothing until something proves it can fail.
!/_negctl_*.py

# Nor the probes. These measure behaviour rather than assert it - the pacing
# probe answers "do the meters actually move over a playthrough", the voice
# probe answers "is this character any good to talk to". Both were force-added
# past the /_* rule at some point without being recorded here, which is how a
# verification tool quietly becomes a single-machine artifact.
!/_*_probe.py

# Nor the boot path, which the same rule swallowed. This is what the Startup
# shortcut actually runs, and it carries the --hub flags that keep this machine
# a client instead of standing up a second diverging Continuum with its own
# database. Losing it loses the desktop client at every login - and a client
# that stops reporting is a budget that silently stops measuring.
!/_autostart.vbs

/@AutomationLog.txt
/$null
